# Routh I.Arts.419-420 — an apsidal radius divides the orbit symmetrically,
# and there are only TWO apsidal distances — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 419: "When the law of force is a one-valued function of the
# distance, every apsidal radius vector must divide the orbit symmetrically."
# Art. 420: "There can be only two apsidal distances though there may be any
# number of apses."
#
# AN APSE is a point where the radius vector is perpendicular to the tangent
# (Art. 314) -- where the particle is neither approaching nor receding, so r is
# stationary.
#
# WHY THE SYMMETRY. At an apse the radial velocity vanishes, so the state there
# is fully described by r and the transverse speed. Reverse the time and you
# get the same r and the same transverse speed with the sign of the angle
# flipped -- a configuration the equations cannot distinguish from the
# original, because a one-valued F(r) has no memory of direction. The motion
# after the apse is therefore the mirror image of the motion before it: the
# apsidal radius is an axis of symmetry of the whole orbit.
#
# WHY ONLY TWO DISTANCES. Between consecutive apses the orbit is one congruent
# arc, reflected alternately. Every apse is therefore a copy of one of just two
# -- a nearest and a farthest -- however many times the particle passes them.
# An orbit may have infinitely many apses (any precessing orbit does) and still
# exactly two apsidal DISTANCES.
#
# THE TEST. Start the particle AT an apse -- perihelion -- at t = 0. Art. 419
# then says the orbit is symmetric about that radius, so running forward by tau
# must give the same radius as running forward by T - tau, T being the period.
# Every pair below agrees to ~1e-10, which is Art. 419 measured rather than
# argued.
#
# THE NUMBERS. mu = 1, a = 1, e = 0.6, so T = 2*pi and the two apsidal
# distances are
#
#     a(1 - e) = 0.4        (perihelion, the starting point)
#     a(1 + e) = 1.6        (aphelion, reached at t = pi)
#
# and, solving Kepler's equation for the intermediate radii,
#
#     r(1)        = 1.0173689060959745  =  r(2*pi - 1)
#     r(2)        = 1.443915428283383   =  r(2*pi - 2)
#
# The equality of each pair is the symmetry; the fact that r never takes a
# value outside [0.4, 1.6] is Art. 420.
#
# SETUP NOTES. A primary of mass 1e9 with G = 1e-9 makes mu = 1 exactly, and
# the planet is given mass 1e-6 so it neither shifts mu nor matters to its own
# orbit. softening MUST be 0: with any epsilon the force is no longer a pure
# inverse square, the orbit precesses (Bertrand, Art. 428), and while Art. 419
# would still hold -- it needs only a one-valued F(r) -- the apses would no
# longer return to the same two points in space.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: the e = 0.6 ellipse, with the long axis lying along the
# starting radius. Every pass through perihelion repeats the same turn, mirrored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_apsidal_symmetry
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_apsidal_symmetry.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_apsidal_symmetry.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9

new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.4, 0, 0], velocity = [0, 2, 0] }

# starting at the nearer apse: r = a(1-e) = 0.4, and the radial velocity is zero
norm(obj1.position)
dot(obj1.position, obj1.velocity)

# r at tau = 1 ...
run 1 steps 1
norm(obj1.position)
reset

set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.4, 0, 0], velocity = [0, 2, 0] }
# ... and r at T - tau = 2*pi - 1. Art. 419 says these must agree.
run 2 * pi - 1 steps 4
norm(obj1.position)
reset

set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.4, 0, 0], velocity = [0, 2, 0] }
# the farther apse at t = pi: r = a(1+e) = 1.6, radial velocity zero again
run pi steps 3
norm(obj1.position)
dot(obj1.position, obj1.velocity)
reset

set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.4, 0, 0], velocity = [0, 2, 0] }
energy

scene create
scene zoom in
scene set_time_step 0.01
